#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1b15864-a555-54fc-a4ed-baf3e1cba297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123455} {:answer/id #uuid "027da969-03c5-5b1d-841f-7c66d2d54a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123456} {:answer/id #uuid "0b3631f1-0f16-55ed-8ccf-2c5e5263ea6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123457} {:answer/id #uuid "331fe688-351c-59a6-9302-13ff4bd4da05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123458} {:answer/id #uuid "717bfdf7-8685-5811-8a1f-846d74216fc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1b15864-a555-54fc-a4ed-baf3e1cba297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123455} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d87c0a-de37-5641-a8c2-e6de7fb6b5fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d2def00-4689-55b3-90a3-9823d892535b" :question/math-academy-id "q-43773" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $K$ containing all constant functions.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $K$ is closed under function addition\n- $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $K$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $K$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n\n*Hint: $f$ is a constant function if $f(x)=\\text{const}$ for all $x\\in [0,1].$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The sum of two constant functions is a constant function. So, $K$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. The product of a constant function by a scalar is again a constant function. So, $K$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $K$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47d40f50-bc19-5575-9aad-fc5fc6b8b5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378600} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcea519-dd0d-5426-91be-2b40b969c072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378601} {:answer/id #uuid "f62c2052-e2a0-535c-86c8-f5709e2f8f70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378602} {:answer/id #uuid "fc66abf8-c4ea-505f-a330-a6435fc686f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378603} {:answer/id #uuid "62f5145f-6879-5927-91db-9243b2eb949f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f5145f-6879-5927-91db-9243b2eb949f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378604} :answer-field/id #uuid "572ecd56-bc1b-5250-8455-9cb9aa201e0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "805bbacf-53a7-54d0-91ca-ce2a2a5f406a" :question/math-academy-id "q-43776" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$ and its subset $L$ containing all invertible $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example if $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=0,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $L.$ So, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0283212e-6a0e-589b-93a3-f2e42e1ba2cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378607} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cf6c35-3bb0-5766-9d03-acdc85b0bf2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378608} {:answer/id #uuid "efe69645-37b1-5bf5-acb2-a01df4ba0aa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378609} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8d172d-c4bc-59c1-bccd-24c6192838b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378610} {:answer/id #uuid "4d776bab-d4cb-5539-990f-c4b7c9ea5d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d776bab-d4cb-5539-990f-c4b7c9ea5d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378611} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c2241f4-3519-5c9e-a609-17c9cde364e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98a80204-c0e6-5c6a-a797-0dc8baa9580c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94625" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $B={[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ 1-a & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $B$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $B$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if ${A}_{1}={A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $k=2,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =2[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a matrix from $B.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is a *not* subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef0db8c2-ab95-532f-afcd-3f4028bf09f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378614} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6d481f-1780-55a1-8d9b-84801d715e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378615} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4b00e6-793e-5623-afe3-47e43a6b4b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378616} {:answer/id #uuid "890afd52-6899-5adf-8229-e279ea7ff62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378617} {:answer/id #uuid "f5873600-8b60-5a63-812d-3f5cd47fa2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5873600-8b60-5a63-812d-3f5cd47fa2ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378618} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b82e59a-6ab5-5153-be4e-52c0d46ac026" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ad169bd-a8be-5b32-a6b6-b79de58801b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94632" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $L$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ is closed under function addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $g(x)=1-x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & =x+(1-x) \\\\  & =(x-x)+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)=x$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot x=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $L$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $L$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378619 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5964b8d3-7311-59b4-9660-776c0cea0b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378621} {:answer/id #uuid "28648013-5436-5d0e-852c-902895516247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378622} {:answer/id #uuid "de786b2e-9f94-5b60-a908-7a17339cd696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378623} {:answer/id #uuid "e43f7512-5288-541e-8576-8f4597dd972b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378624} {:answer/id #uuid "a67a6a3c-f16b-5789-ae26-1fcd23ada4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a67a6a3c-f16b-5789-ae26-1fcd23ada4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378625} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1fa9629-239c-5963-8b4a-a51520a63bb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9702aa0e-9262-5db2-baf6-0c28b2c26439" :question/math-academy-id "q-94635" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $C$ containing all polynomials of degree exactly $3.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is closed under function addition\n- $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $C$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $C$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=1-{x}^{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x) & ={x}^{3}+(1-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-{x}^{3})+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $C$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $C$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""}]}
